course: space
lesson: 10
title: Jupiter
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Jupiter is the king of the planets: about 11 Earths would fit across it, and it is more than twice as heavy as all the other planets put together.
explain: It is a gas giant made mostly of hydrogen and helium, like the Sun, with no ground to stand on: a spaceship sinking into it would only meet thicker and hotter gas, and deep down hydrogen squeezed into liquid metal.
explain: Jupiter spins in under 10 hours, faster than any planet, and that fast spin and its winds stretch its clouds into the light and dark stripes you see in pictures.
explain: Its Great Red Spot is a storm wider than the Earth that has raged for at least 190 years, longer than anyone's great-great-grandparents have lived.
explain: Jupiter's huge gravity acts like a pinball flipper for comets and asteroids, sometimes flinging them away from the inner planets and sometimes towards them.
fact: Jupiter is the largest planet, about 11 times wider than the Earth and more than twice as massive as all the other planets put together.
fact: Jupiter is a gas giant made mostly of hydrogen and helium, like the Sun, and has no solid surface to stand on.
fact: Deep inside Jupiter, hydrogen is squeezed so hard that it becomes a liquid metal that conducts electricity.
fact: Jupiter spins faster than any other planet, once in just under 10 hours.
fact: Jupiter's stripes are bands of clouds, light zones and darker belts, made of ammonia and other chemicals, stretched out by fast winds.
fact: The Great Red Spot is a giant storm on Jupiter that has lasted at least 190 years and is wider than the Earth, though it has been slowly shrinking.
fact: Jupiter is about five times further from the Sun than the Earth and takes about 12 Earth years to orbit once.
fact: Jupiter's gravity flings many comets and asteroids around, sometimes away from the inner planets and sometimes towards them.
fact: In 1994 pieces of the comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 crashed into Jupiter, leaving dark scars bigger than the Earth.
fact: Jupiter has faint, thin rings made of dust, discovered by Voyager 1 in 1979.
fact: Jupiter is surrounded by belts of deadly radiation, the strongest of any planet, which can damage spacecraft electronics.
fact: Jupiter has the most powerful auroras in the Solar System, glowing at its poles in ultraviolet light.
fact: Jupiter is mostly hydrogen like the Sun, but it is far too small to start fusion, so it is not a failed star in any real sense; it would need about 80 times more mass.
exercise: Which is the largest planet? | Jupiter | Saturn | The Earth | Neptune
exercise: How much wider is Jupiter than the Earth? | About 11 times | About 2 times | About 109 times | About the same
exercise: How heavy is Jupiter compared with all the other planets together? | More than twice as massive as all of them | About half as massive as all of them put together | Exactly the same | A hundred times lighter
exercise: What is Jupiter mostly made of? | Hydrogen and helium | Rock and iron | Water and ice | Oxygen and nitrogen
exercise: Could you stand on Jupiter? | No, it has no solid surface | Yes, on its hard, rocky crust below | Yes, but only on its frozen ice | Yes, right at the Great Red Spot
exercise: What happens to hydrogen deep inside Jupiter? | It is squeezed into a liquid metal | It freezes solid into a big ball of ice | It burns like a fire, giving off light | It is squashed until it turns into rock
exercise: How long does Jupiter take to spin once? | Just under 10 hours | About 24 hours, like us | About 12 years, like its orbit | About 243 days, like Venus
exercise: What are Jupiter's stripes? | Bands of ammonia clouds stretched by fast winds | Its huge rings, seen edge-on from the side as dark lines | Rivers on its surface | Shadows of its moons
exercise: What is the Great Red Spot? | A giant storm wider than the Earth | A huge red volcano that is still erupting | A red moon passing in front of Jupiter | A wide lake of red-hot melted rock and iron
exercise: How long has the Great Red Spot lasted? | At least 190 years | About 19 days | Since last year | About 4 billion years
exercise: How long does Jupiter take to orbit the Sun? | About 12 Earth years | About 1 Earth year | About 88 Earth days | About 165 Earth years
exercise: How far is Jupiter from the Sun compared with the Earth? | About five times further | About twice as far | About the same | About fifty times further
exercise: How does Jupiter's gravity affect comets and asteroids? | It flings them around, away from or towards the inner planets | It always pulls them safely into itself | It has no effect on them | It always throws them straight at the Earth
exercise: Who discovered Jupiter's rings, and when? | Voyager 1 in 1979 | Galileo in 1610 | Cassini in 2004 | Apollo 11 in 1969
exercise: What are Jupiter's rings made of? | Faint dust | Bright water ice | Solid rock chunks | Liquid metal
exercise: Why is Jupiter dangerous for spacecraft? | Its radiation belts can damage electronics | Its many moons shoot rocks at any passing spacecraft | It is far too cold out there for metal to work | Its rings block their radio signals back to Earth
exercise: Where does Jupiter have powerful auroras? | At its poles, in ultraviolet light | All over its equator, in green light | Only on its moon Io | Nowhere, auroras are only on the Earth
test: Jupiter's a failed star, it nearly became a second Sun, right? | No, it would need about 80 times more mass to start fusion | Yes, it missed by only a little | Yes, it was a star that burnt out | No, it is made of rock, not gas
test: In a cartoon, an astronaut lands on Jupiter and plants a flag. What is wrong? | Jupiter has no solid ground; the gas just gets thicker and hotter | Nothing, Jupiter has a rocky surface | Flags are not allowed in space | The flag would melt in the cold
test: The Earth spins in 24 hours, but Jupiter, which is far bigger, spins in under 10. What does this mean for a point on Jupiter's equator? | It moves far faster than a point on Earth's equator | It moves more slowly than a point on Earth, because it is bigger | It does not move at all | It moves backwards
test: If the Great Red Spot moved onto the Earth, what would happen? | It would cover more than the whole Earth | It would fit inside one country | It would be about the size of a big city like London | It would cover only the Moon
test: How many birthdays would a 12-year-old on Earth have had in Jupiter years? | About one | About twelve | About 144 | About a hundred
test: Why do people call Jupiter a cosmic pinball flipper? | Its gravity flings comets and asteroids on new paths | It spins like a ball in a machine | It bounces light off its rings like the lights in a pinball machine | Its moons knock each other about
test: Jake: "Jupiter protects us, it catches every asteroid!" What is more accurate? | It flings some away but can send others towards us too | He is right, it catches all of them | It has no effect at all | It throws every asteroid at Mars
test: In 1994 a comet broke into pieces and crashed into Jupiter. What did telescopes see? | Dark scars bigger than the Earth in its clouds | A crater in its rocky surface | Jupiter exploding into rings | Nothing, it swallowed them silently without any marks
test: Why do engineers build Jupiter probes with heavy shielding? | Its radiation belts are the strongest of any planet | Its rings are made of sharp rocks that cut through metal | Its gravity crushes thin metal | Its auroras are made of fire
test: Saturn has huge bright rings. Does Jupiter have rings too? | Yes, faint thin rings of dust, found by Voyager 1 | No, only Saturn has rings | Yes, bigger and brighter than Saturn's, but hidden by clouds | Yes, made of liquid metal
test: Why does Jupiter look like a ball of soft stripes and not like a cratered rock? | We see only the tops of its clouds, stretched into bands by fast winds | Its craters are painted over by rain | It is made of cloth | Its surface is covered with sand dunes
test: Imagine a lift going down into Jupiter. What would it meet on the way? | Ever thicker, hotter gas and then liquid metal hydrogen | A rocky floor after a few kilometres | An ocean of water and then sand | Empty space in the middle
exam: Identify the principal constituents of the planet Jupiter. | Hydrogen and helium | Silicate rock and iron | Water ice and ammonia exclusively | Nitrogen and oxygen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Great Red Spot? | A storm of greater width than the Earth, observed for at least one hundred and ninety years | An extinct volcano situated upon a solid crust | A satellite of Jupiter of reddish hue | A transient storm persisting for some days only
exam: Ascertain the period of rotation of Jupiter about its axis. | Somewhat less than ten hours | Approximately twenty-four hours | Approximately twelve years | Approximately two hundred and forty-three days
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mass of Jupiter. | It exceeds twice the combined mass of all other planets | It is equal to that of the Earth | It is sufficient for nuclear fusion to have commenced | It is less than that of Saturn
